// Credential dumping detection (CrowdStrike)
//
// What it looks for: high and critical CrowdStrike detections mapped to the
// Credential Access tactic, or to T1003 (OS Credential Dumping) specifically.
// Projects the fields that matter for triage — the account, the device, the
// binary, its path and hash, and the full command line.
//
// Why it matters: credential dumping is the pivot point of most intrusions. An
// attacker who reaches LSASS stops needing to guess passwords and starts using
// real ones, which means the next stage of activity looks like legitimate
// authentication. Catching it here is the difference between one compromised
// host and a domain-wide problem.
//
// Why these fields: Cmdline and Filepath are what separate a real finding from
// a false positive. A signed Windows binary running from System32 is normal;
// the same binary invoked with arguments that read process memory is not.
// Sha256 gives you something to scope across the environment and submit to
// threat intel.
//
// What a hit means: treat as a true positive until disproven. Confirm the
// parent process, check whether the account has logged on anywhere else since,
// and scope the hash across every host.
//
// Note on the time filter: ago(1h) suits a scheduled rule. For hunting against
// bulk-ingested lab data, widen or remove it — all Training Lab events share a
// single ingestion timestamp, so a one-hour window returns nothing.

CrowdStrikeDetections
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where Tactic == "Credential Access" or Technique has "T1003"
| where SeverityName in ("High", "Critical")
| project TimeGenerated, Name, Description, SeverityName, Tactic, Technique,
          UserName, SourceAccountUpn, Filename, Filepath, Sha256, Cmdline,
          Objective, Device
